 "result": "{\"answer\": \"Four. The Great Lakes are conventionally counted as five \u2014 Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie, and Ontario \u2014 but Lake Michigan and Lake Huron are hydrologically a single lake (often called \\\"Lake Michigan\u2013Huron\\\"), joined at the Straits of Mackinac, which are about 5 miles wide and 120 feet deep with free exchange of water in both directions and the same surface elevation (~577 ft / 176 m). Counting them as one body gives four Great Lakes: Superior, Michigan\u2013Huron, Erie, and Ontario. On that basis, Michigan\u2013Huron is the largest freshwater lake in the world by surface area (about 45,300 sq mi / 117,300 km\u00b2), surpassing Lake Superior.\", \"confidence\": \"high\", \"abstained\": false}",
